     A Small Internal Signal Marks Thinking-Mode Entry
                  in Open Language Models


                                                  Anonymous Author(s)
                                                      Afﬁliation
                                                       Address
                                                        email



                                                         Abstract
1              Some open language models let users switch “thinking” on or off. We ask whether
2              the activation before generation records that choice and causally selects the re-
3              sponse branch. For three models, a thinking-minus-off vector averaged over the
4              last two tokens of 60 training prompts moves held-out distributions toward think-
5              ing; exact copies localize the effect to response entry. With Qwen3-4B’s soft
6              switch enabled in both modes, one shared branch-token copy selects the donor to-
7              ken on 24/24 localization tests. Widths 1, 2, 4, and 8 each work on 72/72 held-out
8              tests (width-1 controls: 0/432), and one copied token sets donor response format
9              on 72/72 continuations. On 30 non-math prompts and ten seeds, one Nemotron
10             vector decision yields an exact thinking opening on 293/300 tests versus 0/300 ran-
11             dom. On all 30 AIME 2026 problems at a shared 32,768-token cap, the same one-
12             step intervention enters thinking on 30/30 and scores 16/30, versus 1/30 off, 2/30
13             random, and 15/30 canonical thinking. It wins 15–0 against off (p = 6.10 × 10−5 )
14             and 14–0 against random (p = 1.22 × 10−4 ); ﬁxed versus thinking is 4–3 (p = 1).
15             Successful-entry and all-row comparisons coincide. This fresh cap-only rerun fol-
16             lowed an 8K truncation audit; all other choices were unchanged. Manual review
17             covers 120/120 outputs with no strict-scoring false positive or parser error. Re-
18             peated intervention corrupts text. The activation selects entry and can change later
19             answer quality in this paired run, but does not establish continued residual control
20             or seed-averaged accuracy.


21   1 Introduction
22   Open language models increasingly expose a “thinking” button, a template option, or a system
23   instruction that changes how the model answers. These controls affect latency, cost, response length,
24   and answer style. But a visible <think> tag or a long response does not by itself show what changed
25   inside the model.
26   Three things must be kept separate:
27          1. the visible text or setting that selects a mode,
28          2. the internal activation signal present just before generation, and
29          3. the later response, including its length, style, and accuracy.
30   This paper studies the second item. We ask whether the mode choice leaves a measurable internal
31   signal at the end of the prompt and whether changing that signal changes which response style starts.
32   We do not claim that this signal is reasoning itself.
33   The basic test is simple. For the same user prompt, run the model once with thinking on and once
34   with it off. At the last two prompt tokens, subtract the off-mode activations from the on-mode

     Submitted to 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026). Do not distribute.
35   activations. Average that difference over public training prompts to obtain one model-speciﬁc mode
36   vector, denoted cm . Freeze the vector, add it to held-out off-mode activations, and check whether the
37   model’s next-token probabilities move toward those of the held-out thinking run. The intervention
38   sees the held-out off-mode state and the training vector only; the held-out thinking activation is used
39   only afterward for scoring. We call this a prompt-end mode-signal audit.
40   This question is related to token-level reasoning triggers. Mid-Think shows that visible strings such
41   as Okay, </think>, and newline patterns can change response style (Yang et al., 2026). Here the
42   visible switch is held ﬁxed and audited: we directly modify the activation signal it produced. The
43   main result is that several public switches write a small, causal signal at the end of the prompt. The
44   important limitation is equally simple: entering a response mode is not the same as producing a
45   correct or stable long response.

46   Contributions. This paper makes ﬁve contributions.
47          • We introduce a held-out activation test for whether a public thinking switch writes a recov-
48            erable mode signal at the end of the prompt.
49          • We show that ﬁxed, model-speciﬁc mode vectors learned from 60 public paired prompts
50            transfer to held-out prompts in Qwen3.5-4B, Gemma 4 E2B-it, and Nemotron Nano 4B.
51          • We localize the causal signal to the last prompt tokens in Qwen3.5 and Nemotron. In
52            Qwen3-4B, we use the public soft switch and localize causal control to the shared <think>
53            state immediately before the modes diverge. Controllers learned on 12 prompts transfer on
54            36 further prompts; a width sweep shows that the one shared position is sufﬁcient. Copying
55            the full post-attention residual state reproduces the donor effect; copying only the MLP
56            update does not reproduce the full effect.
57          • We test simpler explanations using reversed and random vectors, earlier-token copies, al-
58            tered sufﬁxes, masked opening tokens, and misleading user instructions.
59          • We replicate Qwen3’s selected branch token on 12 disjoint, category- and template-matched
60            prompts with three sampling seeds; test Nemotron generation on held-out non-math
61            prompts and exact copies on six math prompts; and test one-step answer quality on all
62            30 AIME 2026 problems. An 8K truncation audit motivated a fresh 32K rerun of all 120
63            rows with every other choice unchanged. We release row-level results and independent
64            manual, recomputation, and continuation audits.

