93    The layer scans use different grids. Qwen3.5 tests every residual-stream boundary from 0 through 32.
94    Qwen3 tests boundaries 0, 4, . . . , 32, and 36; Nemotron and OLMo test 0, 4, . . . , 32. Boundary 0 is
95    the embedding output passed into the ﬁrst transformer block. Tables report the ﬁrst tested boundary
96    where every prompt moves closer to the donor. For Qwen3.5 this is the earliest boundary in a dense
97    scan. For the other models it is only the ﬁrst successful boundary on a four-layer grid, not an exact
98    onset or a unique causal source.
99    Throughout the paper, intervention labels are written as donor mode into target mode. For example,
100   “think into no-think” means that activations are copied from a thinking run and inserted into the
101   corresponding no-think run before comparing next-token probabilities.

102   Patch metric. Let pD and pT be the donor and target ﬁnal next-token distributions for the same
103   user prompt under two modes, and let pP be the distribution after patching target residual state with
104   donor information. We report donor win when
                                                     JS(pP , pD ) < JS(pP , pT ),
105   where JS is Jensen–Shannon divergence, a bounded measure of how different two probability dis-
106   tributions are. A donor win therefore means that the modiﬁed run is closer to the intended mode
107   than to its unmodiﬁed mode. We also report margin retention for low-rank patches: the fraction of
108   the exact-copy improvement retained by the smaller patch. Conﬁdence intervals resample the stored
109   prompts. They do not cover new checkpoints or all possible prompts, and exact rates such as 60/60
110   are ﬁnite-suite results rather than population guarantees.

111   Fixed mode vector. The main reusable intervention is one model-speciﬁc vector, cm , that moves
112   a prompt-end activation toward thinking mode:
                                                         htail ← htail + αcm .
113   For the three original ﬁxed-vector models, cm is the average thinking-minus-off difference at the
114   ﬁnal two prompt tokens over 60 training prompts. For Qwen3-4B, separate width-1/2/4/8 mean
115   residual tensors are learned from 12 prompts at boundary 28, added for off-to-think, and subtracted
116   for think-to-off. Each scale is selected on those 12 prompts before evaluating the same 36 held-out
117   prompts. A held-out test uses only the target activation and frozen tensor; the donor activation is
118   used afterward for scoring.

119   Exact activation copies. For a layer boundary ℓ and width w, an exact tail patch replaces the
120   target activation at the ﬁnal w prompt positions with the activation observed in the donor run. This is
121   stronger than the ﬁxed-vector test because it uses the matching donor run. Its purpose is localization:
122   matched controls copy the same number of earlier tokens. A separate asymmetric test copies either
123   the full residual state after attention or only the MLP update.

