132   At held-out evaluation time, we add α∆ ¯ d to the target activation; α is selected on training prompts
133   only. The intervention uses the held-out target activation and the frozen training vector, but no
134   held-out thinking-mode activation or difference. For the cross-family rows, a prompt counts as
135   effective only when the modiﬁed distribution moves toward the donor by a JS margin above 0.1 and
136   ends within 0.6 JS of it. This prevents tiny movements near the maximum possible distance from
137   counting as successes.

138   3 The visible switch creates a short-lived internal signal
139   The ﬁrst Qwen experiments test whether the public switch is serialized in the chat template and
140   whether the resulting transition states are distinguishable from arbitrary sufﬁx noise. On 60 prompts,
141   the no-think sufﬁx has a deterministic transient path: immediately before the explicit close, the
142   model predicts </think> on 60/60 prompts; immediately after the close, it predicts the blank sep-
143   arator on 60/60 prompts. The full no-think sufﬁx then moves to direct-answer openers such as To,
144   Let, and Here, while think mode moves to thinking-preface openers such as Thinking and Here.
145   Matched controls separate the complete template sequence from generic sensitivity to a changed
146   sufﬁx. On 180 Qwen prompts spanning ten categories, a real </think> outside the empty-block
147   sequence is close to no-think but not identical (mean JS 0.0534). Fake close tags and moved-close
148   controls do not reproduce the canonical mode. Same-length Okay, Think, and whitespace sufﬁxes
149   also remain far from thinking mode (mean JS 0.6519, 0.6767, and 0.6618). The full serialized
150   sequence matters; merely changing the ﬁnal tokens is insufﬁcient.
151   Three further audits test simpler explanations. First, Qwen’s ﬁxed-vector suite includes calendar,
152   coding, dynamic programming, language, logic, and planning prompts as well as math. On the
153   non-math categories, it reaches donor win 102/108 for no-think into think and 94/108 in reverse; on
154   math, counting, and probability it reaches 72/72 in both directions.
155   Second, Table 4 removes likely mode-signaling tokens before computing JS. The ﬁxed mode vec-
156   tor remains 10/10 in both directions after removing special and whitespace tokens, common think-
157   ing/direct openers, and a broader hand-written list. A verbose-versus-concise style vector falls to
158   chance. With a learned mask, the no-think-into-think vector remains 9/10 after removing the 10
159   most mode-predictive tokens, 8/10 after 100, and 9/10 after 250. Three norm-matched random
160   vectors never exceed 3/10 and move in the wrong direction on average.
161   Third, user instructions are made deliberately misleading. Across 10 held-out prompts, seven prompt
162   variants, and both modes, a classiﬁer using the ﬁnal activation identiﬁes the actual template mode in
163   140/140 cases. A heuristic using the visible instruction is correct in 60/140; a heuristic using the ﬁrst
164   predicted token is correct in 107/140. The signal is therefore not explained by an ordinary request
165   for verbosity or a single opening token.

