177   After localizing the Qwen3 soft-switch decision, we ﬁt controllers of widths 1, 2, 4, and 8 on the
178   same 12 prompts at boundary 28. Each selects the donor token on 72/72 held-out directional tests.
179   Width 1 is sufﬁcient and has 0/432 control wins. Wider deterministic controls also have zero wins;
180   one width-8 random tensor wins on 21/36 prompts because that span reaches the switch instruction.
181   No held-out donor activation or difference enters any controller. Appendix B gives every denomina-
182   tor.
183   The module-labelled tests are asymmetric. The historical attention_only condition copies the
184   donor’s full residual state after attention, including the donor layer input, and then runs the MLP.
185   The mlp_only condition keeps the target’s post-attention state and copies only the donor MLP
186   update. In Qwen3.5-4B, the post-attention-state copy reaches donor win 1.000 in both directions;
187   the MLP-update copy reaches 0.000 in one direction and at most 0.167 in the other. At Qwen3-4B’s
188   soft-switch branch point and in Nemotron, post-attention-state copies reach 1.000 in both directions
189   while MLP-update copies remain 0.000. Thus the post-attention state reproduces the full effect
190   while the isolated MLP update does not reproduce it fully. This does not isolate the attention update
191   or one attention head.

192   5 How the signal is written and read
193   Three tests give a simple picture. First, the signal is present at response entry: exact ﬁnal-prompt
194   copies work in Qwen3.5 and Nemotron, and both exact copying and held-out ﬁxed controllers work
195   at Qwen3-4B’s shared soft-switch branch token. Second, Qwen3 width 1 succeeds on 72/72 held-
196   out tests at the branch point and 0/72 at a non-overlapping earlier position; larger widths do not

