201   6 Transfer beyond the training prompts

202   The main non-AIME test learns ﬁxed vectors on 60 public GSM8K/MATH-500 prompts and evalu-
203   ates them on instructions, coding, factual questions, multiturn conversations, non-English prompts,
204   and GPQA Diamond. Gemma succeeds on 30/30 broad prompts (JS-to-donor 0.0204; random vec-
205   tors 0/90). Nemotron succeeds on 30/30 (JS-to-donor 0.0001; random 0/90), and a reduced broad-
206   plus-GPQA test succeeds on 42/42; moving the same vector to earlier positions gives 0/42. Qwen3-
207   4B’s width-1 branch controller succeeds on 72/72 held-out directional tests, including 48/48 in four
208   categories absent from ﬁtting; widths 2, 4, and 8 do not improve this maximum. Qwen3.5’s broader
209   width-2 ﬁxed vector transfers less consistently; Section 11 reports that boundary.


210   7 Held-out contest-prompt test

211   We ﬁrst test all 60 AIME 2025 and 2026 questions as a disjoint source of prompt text; this boundary
212   audit does not generate or grade their answers. The original localization copies the ﬁnal two prompt-
213   token states, so the ﬁxed controller inherits width 2 before held-out generation; answer quality is
214   not used to choose it. Layer is also ﬁxed before held-out evaluation. The scale α is chosen on the
215   60 training prompts by prioritizing the number of effective prompts, then donor wins, JS margin,
216   and JS-to-donor. No AIME prompt, answer, thinking-mode activation, or mode difference enters
217   that selection. Table 7 then adds the frozen vector to each held-out off-mode activation. The later
218   AIME 2026 generation test uses the already-frozen Nemotron controller and reports answer quality
219   separately.
220   The three model-speciﬁc vectors succeed on 180/180 held-out prompt boundaries. Reversing their
221   signs gives 0/180. Three norm-matched random vectors per model give 19/540, all in the noisier
222   Qwen3.5 row. These are prompt-boundary mode results, not AIME accuracy results.

223   Secondary OLMo checkpoint-pair result. OLMo 3 7B compares separately trained Think and
224   Instruct checkpoints. The same ﬁxed-vector protocol reaches 60/60 held-out prompts toward Think
225   (mean JS-to-donor 0.0207) and 60/60 toward Instruct (0.2873). This secondary result shows that
226   the test can ﬁnd an analogous activation difference across checkpoints; it is not evidence about a
227   same-checkpoint public switch.

                                                                   7
      Table 8: Cross-validated low-rank residual subspace results. CIs are prompt-bootstrap intervals.
      Random subspaces of matched rank achieve donor win 0.000 in all rows.

          Model           Donor into target     Rank       Donor win              Margin retention
          Qwen3.5-4B      think into no-think   2          1.000 [1.000, 1.000]   0.966 [0.906, 1.046]
          Qwen3.5-4B      no-think into think   2          1.000 [1.000, 1.000]   0.910 [0.869, 0.948]
          OLMo 3 7B       think into instruct   2          1.000 [1.000, 1.000]   0.862 [0.811, 0.908]
          OLMo 3 7B       instruct into think   1          1.000 [1.000, 1.000]   1.021 [0.963, 1.074]


228   To summarize the leakage controls: width, layer, and the effective threshold are ﬁxed before the
229   held-out test; only α is selected on training prompts. AIME prompts are disjoint from training, and
230   neither their answers nor their thinking-mode activations are used.


231   8 Low-rank support

232   The ﬁxed-vector audit above is the main deployable prompt-boundary test. A separate low-rank
233   subspace analysis asks a more mechanistic question: whether the exact held-out mode difference
234   lies in a shared low-dimensional causal subspace. Table 8 gives that result. We run 3-fold category-
235   balanced cross-validation over a 60-prompt suite; each fold trains on 40 prompts and evaluates on
236   20 held-out prompts. The learned spectrum is concentrated in Qwen, where the top two components
237   explain 0.874 and 0.854 of donor-target tail-difference energy in the two directions. OLMo has
238   a similarly compressed paired-checkpoint subspace. The subspace interventions transfer causally;
239   unlike the ﬁxed-vector audit, they use the held-out prompt’s exact donor-target difference to choose
240   coordinates inside the train-learned subspace, so they support the low-rank mechanism rather than
241   ﬁxed-vector deployability.
242   A larger Qwen-only 180-prompt subspace suite clariﬁes what the code represents. Full width-2
243   patches have donor win 1.000 with JS to donor near zero. Rank-2 SVD patches also reach donor
244   win 1.000 in both directions; matched random rank-2 subspaces remain at donor win 0.000. Wrong-
245   prompt exact-difference patches transfer strongly, reaching donor win 0.956 and 0.994 in the two
246   directions. The branch code is therefore shared rather than prompt-keyed, consistent with the ﬁxed-
247   vector result above.
248   Logit attribution of the rank-2 Qwen code is branch-vocabulary-shaped. The ﬁrst component in
249   the think-donor direction promotes tokens such as Thinking, thinking, and user-analysis tokens,
250   while suppressing direct-answer openers such as To, To, and ###. This ties the residual subspace
251   to the visible response program rather than leaving it as an abstract donor-win vector.


252   9 Generated behavior diagnostics

253   AIME 2026 answer quality after one entry decision. With the same 32,768-token cap for all
254   four conditions, the ﬁxed intervention enters <think> on 30/30 AIME 2026 problems and scores
255   16/30, versus 1/30 off, 2/30 random, and 15/30 canonical thinking. It beats off on 15 problems with
256   no losses (p = 6.10 × 10−5 ) and random on 14 with no losses (p = 1.22 × 10−4 ); against thinking
257   it has four wins and three losses (p = 1). Entry succeeds on every ﬁxed row, so restricting the
258   analysis to successful entries changes nothing. Fixed completes 26/30 and caps on 4/30, compared
259   with 23/30 and 7/30 for thinking.
260   The width-2 Nemotron vector was learned only on 60 GSM8K/MATH-500 prompts, then ﬁxed at
261   boundary 28 with α = 4. It changes only the ﬁrst generated decision; later generation is ordinary.
262   After the 8,192-token run truncated 65/120 rows, we committed a cap-only correction and generated
263   all 120 rows again from scratch. Every other choice was unchanged; the correction is post-hoc, not
264   preregistered. A hash-bound manual audit covers all 120 outputs and ﬁnds no strict-scoring false
265   positive or parser error. All 55 old completed rows reproduce exactly, all 65 old cap hits share the
266   exact 8,192-token preﬁx, and zero previously correct row regresses. This shows that one causally
267   selected entry token can change later answer quality in this paired benchmark run. The one seed
268   does not establish seed-averaged accuracy. Appendix F.4 gives complete tables and audits.

                                                       8
269   Other follow-through tests. One copied Qwen3 branch token sets donor response format on 72/72
270   continuations, while all 72 controls retain the target format. One Nemotron vector decision produces
271   an exact <think> opening on 293/300 generations versus 0/300 for random vectors. Reapplying
272   either intervention degrades or corrupts text. Appendix F gives the complete protocols and audits.

273   10    Related work
274   Hybrid-reasoning work studies when to spend reasoning budget and how visible triggers or steering
275   vectors change response style (Jiang et al., 2025; Yang et al., 2026; Venhoff et al., 2025). We instead
276   test the internal prompt-end signal written by a public switch, separating exact copies from vectors
277   learned on other prompts. Causal-rationale work studies later reasoning traces (Rahim and Rahim,
278   2025; Sathyanarayanan et al., 2026); we study the state before a long trace exists. We build on
279   activation patching and layerwise readout (Geva et al., 2023; Belrose et al., 2023; Zhang and Nanda,
280   2024), adding reversed, random, earlier-position, token-mask, sufﬁx, and misleading-instruction
281   controls.

282   11    Limitations
283   We primarily measure response entry, not faithful reasoning. The 60-row AIME boundary audit does
284   not generate answers. The separate AIME 2026 answer test uses one paired seed over 30 problems;
285   its bootstrap measures task, not sampling-seed, variation.
286   The vectors are model-speciﬁc. Qwen3.5, Gemma, and Nemotron use width-2 tails. Qwen3-4B
287   succeeds at widths 1–8, but only at the ﬁrst branch decision; width 8 reaches switch text and has a
288   random-control win. OLMo uses separate checkpoints, and Gemma’s reverse ﬁxed vector is unreli-
289   able.
290   OLMo, Phi, and Liquid compare paired checkpoints, not same-checkpoint switches; Liquid is asym-
291   metric. Models without paired modes require a different experiment.
292   Transfer varies: Gemma and Nemotron succeed broadly, while Qwen3.5 reaches the strict threshold
293   on 13/30 broad prompts versus 2/90 random controls.
294   Generated answers are harder because each token changes what follows. At 32K, ﬁxed enters think-
295   ing on 30/30 and scores 16/30, versus 1/30 off, 2/30 random, and 15/30 thinking. It is signiﬁcantly
296   better than off and random in this paired task sample but not distinguishable from thinking. Fixed
297   completes 26/30 and thinking 23/30; the remaining 4 and 7 rows hit the common cap. This is one
298   paired seed over 30 problems, and the bootstrap varies tasks rather than sampling seeds. It shows
299   that entry control changes later answer quality in this run, but it does not establish continued residual
300   control, universal reasoning recovery, or a seed-averaged gain. Reverse entry also misses canonical
301   direct behavior, and repeated interventions corrupt text.
302   Localization remains partial: tail post-attention residual copies work where isolated MLP updates
303   do not, but this isolates neither attention nor one head. We ﬁnd no cross-family vector or benchmark-
304   solving policy, and proprietary switches may differ.

305   12    Conclusion
306   Public switches leave causal entry signals. Qwen3-4B controllers of widths 1–8 select 72/72 held-
307   out donor tokens, and one copied token sets 72/72 continuation formats. Fixed vectors also transfer
308   in Qwen3.5, Gemma, and Nemotron. On AIME 2026, Nemotron’s ﬁxed entry succeeds on 30/30
309   and scores 16/30 at 32K, versus 1/30 off, 2/30 random, and 15/30 thinking. It signiﬁcantly beats
310   off and random in this paired sample and is not distinguishable from thinking. Entry is controllable
311   and can change later answer quality; continued residual control and seed-averaged recovery remain
312   open.

