357   B Qwen3 width sweeps and ﬁxed-controller audit details
358   B.1    Earlier exact-copy width sweeps

359   Two earlier 12-prompt experiments tested widths 1, 2, 4, and 8 using the matching donor activation
360   for each prompt. Table 10 reports them because they motivated the cleaner branch-point design,
361   not because they establish held-out controller transfer. The ﬁrst used the hard chat-template switch,
362   enable_thinking=True versus False; its serialized prompts differ by a four-token assistant sufﬁx.
363   The second kept enable_thinking=True and used /think versus /no_think, but patched the
364   ordinary serialized prompt tail before adding the shared <think> token. In that soft-switch sweep,
365   non-tail width-2/4/8 copies also reach donor win 1.0, so it does not uniquely localize the effect to
366   the tail. The ﬁrst four prompt positions never win in either direction.

                                  Switch and copied block Direction            Width 1   Width 2   Width 4   Width 8
                                  Hard template, tail          off to think        20         0         0         0
                                  Hard template, tail          think to off        28         4         0         8
                                  Hard template, non-tail      off to think         –      none      none      none
                                  Hard template, non-tail      think to off         –      none      none      none
                                  Soft instruction, tail       off to think        32        28        24          0
                                  Soft instruction, tail       think to off        32        24        24          0
                                  Soft instruction, non-tail   off to think         –         8         0          0
                                  Soft instruction, non-tail   think to off         –         8         0          0
      Table 10: Earlier Qwen3-4B exact-copy width sweeps. Entries are the ﬁrst tested residual boundary
      with donor wins on all 12 prompts; the grid is 0, 4, . . . , 36, “none” means no tested boundary, and “–”
      means the block was not tested. Every tail width reaches 12/12 at some boundary in both directions.
      These are same-prompt donor copies, not ﬁxed controllers, and have no ﬁt/held-out split.


367   B.2    Held-out ﬁxed-controller width sweep

368   The new width protocol, runner, prompt IDs, layer boundary, alpha grid, selection rule, controls,
369   random seeds, and interpretation limits were committed before inference. Both conditions use model
370   revision 4dcb3d101c2a062e5c1d4bb173588c54ea6c4d25, keep enable_thinking=True, and
371   differ only through the public /think and /no_think instructions. We supply the same <think>

                                                                              12
372   token, then audit the ﬁrst divergent next-token decision. For each width, the frozen controller is the
373   mean thinking-minus-off residual tensor over the same 12 ﬁt prompts at boundary 28. The ﬁt-only
374   selection rule chooses α = 1 for every width. Evaluation uses 12 new values from the same six
375   authored categories and 24 prompts from four categories absent from ﬁtting. It never uses a held-out
376   donor activation or difference.

                               Width α Fixed wins Token matches Zero Reversed Earlier span Random
                                  1    1       72/72      72/72   0/72       0/72         0/72       0/216
                                  2    1       72/72      72/72   0/72       0/72         0/72       0/216
                                  4    1       72/72      72/72   0/72       0/72         0/72       0/216
                                  8    1       72/72      72/72   0/72       0/72         0/72      21/216
      Table 11: Held-out Qwen3-4B ﬁxed-controller width sweep over 36 prompts and both directions.
      Every ﬁxed row comprises 24 same-category and 48 category-shift directional tests. The earlier-span
      control has the same width but never overlaps the active response-entry span. Random denominators
      contain three preregistered norm-matched tensors. All 21 random wins are one width-8 seed in the
      off-to-think direction.


377   Width 1 already reaches the maximum binary result, so more positions add no success beneﬁt. Its
378   mean JS-to-donor is 8.05 × 10−9 off-to-think and 9.94 × 10−7 think-to-off; mean donor margins are
379   0.69315 and 0.69313. Controller norms for widths 1/2/4/8 are 112.587/144.613/201.942/303.778,
380   and the corresponding mean ﬁt-prompt cosines to the mean tensor are 0.975/0.951/0.963/0.960. The
381   tokenizer audit shows that widths 1, 2, and 4 stay within the shared <think> and assistant-header
382   sufﬁx, while width 8 reaches the differently tokenized /think and /no_think instruction frag-
383   ments. This explains why width 1 is the cleanest sufﬁciency and speciﬁcity result. These tests do
384   not measure accuracy, reasoning quality, or later-token control.

