529   The ﬁxed and random vectors differ on every prompt at the ﬁrst generated decision in both directions.
530   The ﬁxed vector produces the donor opening on 29/30 forward prompts versus 0/30 for random
531   (paired exact p = 3.73×10−9 ) and 30/30 reverse prompts versus 0/30 for random (p = 1.86×10−9 ).
532   None of the 60 ﬁxed-vector outputs triggers the pre-speciﬁed repetition test, and none has low token
533   diversity over its ﬁrst 64 tokens. The one forward miss begins with the malformed fragment <think
534   offhand; two reverse outputs enter a think block later.
535   The forward effect continues beyond the opening: its completion count is 15/30 versus 16/30 for
536   canonical thinking, and its mean length differs by −26.0 tokens (95% prompt-bootstrap interval
537   [−78.8, 25.1]). The reverse intervention selects the direct opening but does not restore the full
538   direct-answer trajectory: it completes 12/30 responses versus 24/30 for canonical off (p = .00183)
539   and is 213.3 tokens longer on average (95% interval [124.5, 308.5]). This is reliable entry control,
540   not symmetric control of the complete response.
541   As a stress test, we reapplied the same ﬁxed vector for 64 generated decisions on the same 30
542   prompts. The ﬁrst-step records and canonical baselines are identical to the one-step run, but all 30
543   forward outputs begin with a repeated <th fragment and all 30 ﬁxed-vector outputs in each direction
544   fail the carried-forward ﬁrst-64 diversity test. Nine of 30 reverse outputs also meet the full-output
545   repetition rule. The corruption is caused by repeatedly forcing a prompt-end vector after generation
546   has moved into a different state; it is evidence against using this ﬁxed intervention as a long-horizon
547   controller.

